From: minsky@media.mit.edu (Marvin Minsky)
Date: Wed, 2 Nov 94 12:15:19 -0500
Subject: [#94-11-35] Zinc!

oseiler@unixg.ubc.ca writes:

>I started taking a zinc supplement a couple of weeks ago again, as well
>as a B-complex supplement. I mainly started the zinc up again since it
>has been found to give a good boost to the immune system, which I felt
>I needed (I have been having rather excessive weekends for almost four
>months solid now, and started to feel run down - I got the first cold
>I've had in years. The day after starting my B-complex supplement I
>felt amazing... Felt just great since)

As a skeptic, I'm always on the lookout for concidences.  People often
"feel great" from time to time and attribute this to some recent event, be
it taking a pill, visiting a psychic, or whatever.  One-of-a-kind
anecdotes, not followed by a careful, controlled, skeptical study, could be
the principle cause of the general public confusion.

Of course, when we notice a conjuction, we should consider it as a
hypothesis -- but I don't see any value in announcing it to the world
before doing some more to confirm it, with at least some attempts at
controlled experiments.  I was amused to note that the same writer said
recently:

>To: Extropians@extropy.org
>Date: Tue, 1 Nov 1994 13:37:38 -0800 (PST)
>From: Oliver Seiler <oseiler@unixg.ubc.ca>

[From a thread about Bureaucracy]
>I should explain myself more on this point. Record-keeping and project
>management are two things that I think are necessary and quite useful.
>However, like all good things, more of a good thing isn't necessarily
>better. My rule of thumb is to try to find the optimal balance of
>record-keeping, management, etc. to move an individual project (since
>I am a project-based person) forward....

I say that in the case of health rumors, one should need more of a good thing.
This is simply because positive anecdote collection tend to only confirm
already positive-rumored memes -- (we scarcely ever hear things like "I
stopped taking zinc the other day and felt just great since") -- and to do
the opposite for negative-rumored memes (I walked under a ladder, broke a
mirror, caught a cold on Friday the 13th, etc).

I hasten to add the rest of Oliver's paragraph:

>                                                     ....forward. To me a
>bureaucracy is an
>organization which doesn't address the optimization issue. My general
>feeling is that these organizations eventually stagnate and die, as
>the major amount of energy isn't spent on "useful" work, but on
>"infrastructure" work...

Yes indeed!  I've lived through several of these.  One might add to
Seiler's observations that the real problem comes when those organizations
stagnate and *don't* die!

