Date: Tue, 01 Nov 1994 02:43:51 -0600
Subject: [#94-11-38] Argument by Analogy (Uploads)

At 03:24 AM 11/2/94 EST, extropians@extropy.org wrote:
>Craig Presson wrote 11/1/94: <...the toaster cannot make and defend
>claims or enter into and perform contracts.>
>
>Toasters, that is, machines, can't do this, ok.  

*BZZZT* Tripping over contrapositives, are we?

><"Free will" does not distinguish the class of machines from the class
>of humans, since I can demonstrate simple non-deterministic machines[1], and
>even, in the computer, evolved machines...>
>
>This is a philosophical statement.  Since you maintain that philosophy is
>useless and boring[...]

What I find useless and boring is all these pretty rhetorical capers.
It is a better demonstration than I could make of what is wrong with
treating "philosophical" questions with philosophical approaches. You
can make almost every question into a philosophical one, and that
usually means you can't answer it anymore.

I think of [1] as an operational statement. We can today
design non-deterministic machines with special hardware,
and pretty good imitations thereof in software alone. This
implies that machine artifacts need not be deterministic,
and destroys your toaster analogy. Whatever barrier you set
up, and say, "this a machine cannot do", soon a machine will
do it.

><Show me one thing a philosopher has accomplished in his subject in the last 50
>years.>
[Aside: PGF nominated Popper; I agree that devising a philosophy
of science intelligible and useful to working scientists was a
worthwhile accomplishment. (000001) ]

>It's true that philosophical concepts don't launch themselves onto "Oprah" and
>"Inside Edition" with great speed, often it takes centuries or millenia to
>really sink into a society.  For example, our friend Rousseau's ideas have
>pretty much debilitated Western Civilization [...]

The people who are busy fucking up Western Civilization
use next to nothing from Rousseau himself. I've heard this claim
before, and it seems tenuous to me. While I recognize
that Marx <spit!> was influenced by Hegel who was influenced by
somebody who was ... influenced by Rousseau, you can't apply
set theory to this extremely fuzzy chain and make Hillary Clinton,
Donna Shalala, Jeremy Rifkin, Lester R. Brown, Fidel Castro,
and Deng Shao-Ping into disciples of Rousseau. Why not choose
Kant as the enemy instead, as Ayn Rand did? He was much more
subtle and persuasive than Jean-Jacques.

Even the environmental terrorists share only a simple meme with
Rousseau, which they could have fallen into without him. All I'm
able to see clearly about this implication is that teachers have
lent respectability to Rousseau and his breast-beating by continuing
to teach _Confessions_ as part of the canon, and let generations of
students, with their warm little hearts, horny little glands, and
mushy, romantic little brains, absorb his ideas without vaccination. 

[...]

Now it's infusing them with Rousseauean neo-pagan
>environmentalist irrationality and the Hegelian/Marxist view of life as
>elaborate collective machines, anti-individualistic in the extreme.  Only
>philosophic concepts can reverse this alarming trend, like the Extropian
>Principles, as an example.

In large part the EP and PCR serve as vaccines against memetic invasion
from academic philosophy. I still think philosophies exist to
be outgrown. Someone should step up and write the sequel to
_Zarathustra_, and do to philosophy what FN tried to do to religion.

Now, I know we have other philosophy students and ex-students on this
list. At least one former phil. student is now in CS, one former
phil. professor is a wealthy and influential writer :-) and some
ur-VEPs were slave laborers in Philosophy Department gulags back
around 368 P.N.O., the year of the Extropian Dawn ... I don't 
know whether their silence is ominous or not.

You got anything else in that bag of tricks besides assertions?






